      "decision_index": 132,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Llama4 Libby chose Malevolent Rumble over playing Eldrazi Temple from hand. Playing Eldrazi Temple first is strictly better sequencing - it provides additional mana (especially for colorless Eldrazi spells via the {C}{C} ability) that could be used this turn, and Malevolent Rumble can still be cast afterward with the extra mana available. Additionally, the reasoning given ('to deal damage to my opponent and potentially clear their board') is completely wrong - Malevolent Rumble reveals cards and finds a permanent, it doesn't deal damage or clear boards. The player should play a land before casting spells when possible.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Malevolent Rumble before playing Eldrazi Temple from hand",
      "better_line": "Play Eldrazi Temple first (making the land drop), then cast Malevolent Rumble with more mana available. The extra Eldrazi Temple could help cast Devourer of Destiny or Ugin sooner.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 169,
      "player": "Llama4 Libby",
      "decision_index": 141,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Qwen3 Spike targeted the opponent with Flame of Anor's 'draw two cards' mode, giving the opponent two free cards instead of drawing them themselves. At 5 life facing a 6/6 Devourer of Destiny with a thin hand, drawing two cards was critical for survival. Giving the opponent additional resources in this position is catastrophic.",
